<li class="ltx_tocentry ltx_tocentry_subsection"><a href="#A8.SS4" title="In Appendix H Theoretical Constants: Sources, Mappings, and Practical Calibration ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_title"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_ref">H.4 </span>Three-Step Calibration Procedure</span></a></li>
</ol></li>
</ol></nav>
</nav>
<div class="ltx_page_main">
<div id="infobox" class="infobox">
  <a id="license-tr" href="https://info.arxiv.org/help/license/index.html#licenses-available">
    License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license
  </a>
  <div id="watermark-tr">
arXiv:2509.20677v1 [cs.LG] 25 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Tongxi Wang &amp; Zhuoyang Xia
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>School of Future Technology
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Southeast University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Nanjing, China
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:"><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">{tongxi_wang,zhuoyangxia}@seu.edu.cn</span></a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">In-context learning (ICL) is flexible but its reliability is highly sensitive to prompt length. This paper establishes a non-asymptotic lower bound that links the minimal number of demonstrations to ICL stability under fixed high-dimensional sub-Gaussian representations. The bound gives explicit sufficient conditions in terms of spectral properties of the covariance, providing a computable criterion for practice. Building on this analysis, we propose a two-stage observable estimator with a one-shot calibration that produces practitioner-ready prompt-length estimates without distributional priors. Experiments across diverse datasets, encoders, and generators show close alignment between the predicted thresholds and empirical knee-points, with the theory acting as a conservative but reliable upper bound; the calibrated variant further tightens this gap. These results connect spectral coverage to stable ICL, bridge theory and deployment, and improve the interpretability and reliability of large-scale prompting in realistic finite-sample regimes.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="id2" class="ltx_logical-block ltx_align_bottom">
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:2.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>
    
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_top">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:0.0pt;height:24.0pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In-Context Learning (ICL) has become a central capability of large language models, enabling them to solve novel tasks at test time without parameter updates. Despite its flexibility, the reliability of ICL is highly sensitive to the number of demonstrations provided in the prompt. Practitioners report that the stability threshold varies widely across tasks and models, and naive scaling rules often fail to predict it accurately <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>. This motivates a principled question: what is the minimal number of demonstrations required for stable ICL?</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recent theory provides partial answers. PAC-style analyses study when ICL is learnable and give finite-sample guarantees under mixture assumptions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Algorithmic perspectives view transformers as learners with task-dependent sample complexity, yielding upper bounds rather than minimal requirements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>. Asymptotic learning curves characterize phase transitions in analytically tractable models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Complementary lines of work improve calibration in ICL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, or reveal mechanistic insights into emergent behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. However, none of these studies provide a non-asymptotic, computable <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower bound</em> that directly links prompt length to stability via spectral properties <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we address this gap by theoretically analyzing the relationship between the number of demonstrations in a prompt and the stability of ICL. Assuming the model relies on fixed high-dimensional representations, we derive an explicit non-asymptotic lower bound that characterizes when ICL remains stable with high probability. Building on this analysis, we propose a two-stage observable method with one-shot calibration that operationalizes the result in practice without requiring distributional priors <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are threefold:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide a non-asymptotic theoretical lower bound that, for the first time, establishes a quantitative relationship between the number of in-context demonstrations and the stability of ICL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design an observable two-stage algorithm that estimates the required number of demonstrations in practical tasks while preserving theoretical guarantees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct extensive experiments to validate the consistency between our theoretical predictions and empirical model behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Works</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical analyses.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Prior studies have examined ICL from different theoretical angles. PAC-style formulations establish when ICL is learnable under mixture models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Transformers have also been analyzed as algorithms with task-dependent sample complexity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, while asymptotic learning curves describe phase transitions in analytically tractable settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Task-specific results for regression and structured prediction further refine these analyses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Calibration and reliability.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Calibration-based methods improve few-shot prediction confidence and robustness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>. These approaches are orthogonal to our goal: they refine predictive distributions but do not specify the minimal prompt length for stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Mechanistic and empirical perspectives.</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mechanistic studies connect internal representations to emergent ICL behavior <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>. Empirical benchmarks and toolkits provide standardized pipelines and evaluation practices <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. Independent work observes that empirical knee-points often appear at substantially fewer examples than naive rules suggest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, a phenomenon our theory explains through spectral coverage <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, our analysis complements these lines by supplying a <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">lower-bound perspective</em> that is both non-asymptotic and computable <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience)</h2>

<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Assumptions</h5>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We analyze in-context learning under the following layered assumptions on the feature representation
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A1) Nominal assumption (strong).</span>
A deterministic feature map <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and each <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is sub-Gaussian with zero mean and covariance <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
This assumption is adopted for the main theorem, under which all proofs are rigorous.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A2) Approximate stationarity.</span>
In practice, the representation may depend on context index <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>.
We model <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> (uniformly in <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,t</annotation></semantics></math> or in expectation).
When <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> is small, our lower bound degrades smoothly by an additive correction term <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> (see Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Robustness Extensions ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A3) Moment relaxation.</span>
Instead of sub-Gaussian tails, we allow <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> to be sub-exponential or to satisfy bounded fourth-moment conditions.
Concentration results can be adapted with modified constants, leading to slightly larger sample-size requirements.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(A4) Weak dependence.</span>
When the context induces dependence among features <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assume a mixing or martingale-difference condition.
In this case, matrix concentration inequalities for dependent sequences apply, yielding bounds of the same form up to a dependence factor (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extensions to Dependent Features ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All formal proofs in the main text are carried out under the nominal assumption (A1).
The relaxed settings (A2)–(A4) are analyzed in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Extensions Beyond the Nominal Assumption ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>, where we show that the main results remain valid up to controlled perturbation terms.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A frozen feature map <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi:\mathcal{X}\to\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> is assumed, satisfying <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}\phi(x)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D}}\phi(x)=0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\phi(x)\|_{\psi_{2}}\leq\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\phi(x)\|_{\psi_{2}}\leq\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> (the vector <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-Gaussian norm).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The covariance is <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma=\mathbb{E}[\phi\phi^{\top}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma=\mathbb{E}[\phi\phi^{\top}]\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, with rank <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r=\operatorname{rank}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>rank</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r=\operatorname{rank}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r&lt;d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r&lt;d</annotation></semantics></math> we discuss on the support subspace). Denote the smallest nonzero eigenvalue by <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{r}(\Sigma)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{r}(\Sigma)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and for brevity write <math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)=\lambda_{r}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)=\lambda_{r}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A demonstration sequence <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{K}=\{x_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{K}=\{x_{k}\}_{k=1}^{K}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of i.i.d. draws from <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Define</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{K}=\begin{bmatrix}\phi(x_{1})\\
\vdots\\
\phi(x_{K})\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d},\qquad\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}=\frac{1}{K}H_{K}H_{K}^{\top}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}X_{k}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{K}=\begin{bmatrix}\phi(x_{1})\\
\vdots\\
\phi(x_{K})\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{K\times d},\qquad\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}=\frac{1}{K}H_{K}H_{K}^{\top}=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}X_{k},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}=\phi(x_{k})\phi(x_{k})^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}=\phi(x_{k})\phi(x_{k})^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> is a PSD matrix.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Goal: give the minimal <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> (parameterized by <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma,\sigma,r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma,\sigma,r</annotation></semantics></math>, or provide an observable two-stage estimator) such that</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\big(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta\big)\geq 1-\xi," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\big(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta\big)\geq 1-\xi,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0&lt;\delta&lt;\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0&lt;\delta&lt;\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Basic matrix tail bound (a one-sided matrix Bernstein for the minimum eigenvalue)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Define centered matrices <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y_{k}:=X_{k}-\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y_{k}:=X_{k}-\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>, so <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}Y_{k}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}Y_{k}=0</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\Sigma=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\Sigma=\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We need to control <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})=\lambda_{\min}\!\Big(\Sigma+\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}\Big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})=\lambda_{\min}\!\Big(\Sigma+\frac{1}{K}\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}\Big)</annotation></semantics></math>. By a matrix Bernstein inequality (or a lower-tail form of Tropp-style matrix concentration), there exist constants <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c,C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c,C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for any <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\Big(\lambda_{\min}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}\Big)\leq-t\Big)\leq r_{\mathrm{eff}}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{t^{2}}{2v+\tfrac{2}{3}Rt}\Big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\Big(\lambda_{\min}\Big(\sum_{k=1}^{K}Y_{k}\Big)\leq-t\Big)\leq r_{\mathrm{eff}}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{t^{2}}{2v+\tfrac{2}{3}Rt}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v=\Big\|\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{E}[Y_{k}^{2}]\Big\|=K\cdot\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><munderover><mo lspace="0em" movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></munderover><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v=\Big\|\sum_{k=1}^{K}\mathbb{E}[Y_{k}^{2}]\Big\|=K\cdot\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> is (on a high-probability event) an upper bound for <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{k}\|Y_{k}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>Y</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{k}\|Y_{k}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Set <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=K\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=K\Delta</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta:=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta:=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting into (<a href="#S3.E1" title="In 3.1 Basic matrix tail bound (a one-sided matrix Bernstein for the minimum eigenvalue) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and adjusting scaling yields for any <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\big(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\leq\delta\big)\leq r_{\mathrm{eff}}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{K\Delta^{2}}{2\widetilde{v}+\tfrac{2}{3}R\Delta}\Big)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\big(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\leq\delta\big)\leq r_{\mathrm{eff}}\exp\!\Big(-\frac{K\Delta^{2}}{2\widetilde{v}+\tfrac{2}{3}R\Delta}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{v}:=\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{v}:=\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|</annotation></semantics></math>; the detailed derivation is in the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Controlling parameters under the sub-Gaussian assumption (truncation and fourth-moment bounds)</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1 </span>Fourth-moment bound</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From the vector <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> assumption <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\phi\|_{\psi_{2}}\leq\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>σ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\phi\|_{\psi_{2}}\leq\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> there exists an absolute constant <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\|\phi\|^{4}\leq C_{1}\sigma^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\|\phi\|^{4}\leq C_{1}\sigma^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Hence</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}\|X\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\|\phi\phi^{\top}\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\|\phi\|^{4}\leq C_{1}\sigma^{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}\|X\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\|\phi\phi^{\top}\|^{2}=\mathbb{E}\|\phi\|^{4}\leq C_{1}\sigma^{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Using the operator inequality <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X-\Sigma)^{2}\preceq 2X^{2}+2\Sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⪯</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X-\Sigma)^{2}\preceq 2X^{2}+2\Sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> we get</p>
<table id="S3.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{v}=\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|\leq 2\mathbb{E}\|X\|^{2}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2}\leq 2C_{1}\sigma^{4}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{v}=\big\|\mathbb{E}[(X-\Sigma)^{2}]\big\|\leq 2\mathbb{E}\|X\|^{2}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2}\leq 2C_{1}\sigma^{4}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.2 </span>Truncation to control R</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Typically <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|X_{k}\|=\|\phi(x_{k})\|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|X_{k}\|=\|\phi(x_{k})\|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> is not almost surely bounded, but by the sub-Gaussian tail (there exists <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>)</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(\|\phi\|\geq t)\leq 2\exp\!\Big(-\frac{c_{1}t^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:1.370em;" width="1.370em"><mi>exp</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\|\phi\|\geq t)\leq 2\exp\!\Big(-\frac{c_{1}t^{2}}{\sigma^{2}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Choose threshold <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{0}=C_{2}\sigma\sqrt{\log(8K/\xi)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{0}=C_{2}\sigma\sqrt{\log(8K/\xi)}</annotation></semantics></math> (constant <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> depends on <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1}C_{2}^{2}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1}C_{2}^{2}=1</annotation></semantics></math>). Then for a single sample</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(\|\phi\|&gt;t_{0})\leq\frac{\xi}{4K}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mi>ξ</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\|\phi\|&gt;t_{0})\leq\frac{\xi}{4K}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">By a union bound over the <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> samples, with probability at least <math id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\xi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\xi/4</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{1\leq k\leq K}\|X_{k}\|=\max_{k}\|\phi(x_{k})\|^{2}\leq B_{K}:=C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log\frac{8K}{\xi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>k</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{1\leq k\leq K}\|X_{k}\|=\max_{k}\|\phi(x_{k})\|^{2}\leq B_{K}:=C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log\frac{8K}{\xi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">On this high-probability event we may take</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R=\|X_{k}-\Sigma\|\leq B_{K}+\|\Sigma\|\leq C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log\frac{8K}{\xi}+\|\Sigma\|." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=\|X_{k}-\Sigma\|\leq B_{K}+\|\Sigma\|\leq C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log\frac{8K}{\xi}+\|\Sigma\|.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Substitute into (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Basic matrix tail bound (a one-sided matrix Bernstein for the minimum eigenvalue) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to obtain a sample-size lower bound (one main result)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Substituting (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2.1 Fourth-moment bound ‣ 3.2 Controlling parameters under the sub-Gaussian assumption (truncation and fourth-moment bounds) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2.2 Truncation to control R ‣ 3.2 Controlling parameters under the sub-Gaussian assumption (truncation and fourth-moment bounds) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) into (<a href="#S3.E2" title="In 3.1 Basic matrix tail bound (a one-sided matrix Bernstein for the minimum eigenvalue) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), and treating the rank-related prefactor <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> as a logarithmic factor, require the RHS to be <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\xi-\xi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\xi-\xi/4</annotation></semantics></math>. One obtains a sufficient sample-size lower bound:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. If</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq\left[\frac{2\widetilde{v}}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{2R}{3\Delta}\right]\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq\left[\frac{2\widetilde{v}}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{2R}{3\Delta}\right]\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">then (combining the truncation failure <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi/4</annotation></semantics></math> and Bernstein failure) we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta)\geq 1-\xi." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta)\geq 1-\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Replacing <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{v}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>v</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{v}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R</annotation></semantics></math> by the bounds from (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2.1 Fourth-moment bound ‣ 3.2 Controlling parameters under the sub-Gaussian assumption (truncation and fourth-moment bounds) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In 3.2.2 Truncation to control R ‣ 3.2 Controlling parameters under the sub-Gaussian assumption (truncation and fourth-moment bounds) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) gives the explicit bound</p>
<table id="S3.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\gtrsim\left[\frac{2(2C_{1}\sigma^{4}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2})}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{2\big(C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log(8K/\xi)+\|\Sigma\|\big)}{3\Delta}\right]\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\gtrsim\left[\frac{2(2C_{1}\sigma^{4}+2\|\Sigma\|^{2})}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{2\big(C_{2}^{2}\sigma^{2}\log(8K/\xi)+\|\Sigma\|\big)}{3\Delta}\right]\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where “<math id="S3.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gtrsim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gtrsim</annotation></semantics></math>” hides absolute constants and note <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> appears inside the <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(8K/\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(8K/\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> factor (a self-consistency issue); in practice one can use a single fixed-point iteration or replace <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(8K/\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(8K/\xi)</annotation></semantics></math> by the conservative upper bound <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log(8K_{\max}/\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>max</mi></msub></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log(8K_{\max}/\xi)</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency)</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the higher-level literature one often uses the following operator-norm concentration (Vershynin / Koltchinskii–Lounici style): there exists a constant <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> such that for a sub-Gaussian vector</p>
<table id="S3.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr\Big(\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\Sigma\|\geq C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}\Big)\Big)\leq\xi." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr\Big(\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K}-\Sigma\|\geq C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}\Big)\Big)\leq\xi.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Using (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) to ensure <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (appendix explains why this suffices) we require</p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}\Big)\leq\Delta." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}\Big)\leq\Delta.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Ignoring the lower-order <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mi>K</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{K}</annotation></semantics></math> term (negligible when <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> is large), we get the simpler leading sample-size term:</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq C^{\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\Delta^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq C^{\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\Delta^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is a constant (<math id="S3.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}=C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}=C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>). This is the common practical form.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>A two-stage observable (plug-in) scheme and finite-sample guarantee</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The bounds in (<a href="#S3.E8" title="In 3.3 Substitute into () to obtain a sample-size lower bound (one main result) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) depend on unknowns <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma,\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma),\|\Sigma\|,r_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma,\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma),\|\Sigma\|,r_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We now give an observable two-stage scheme and show it works in finite samples.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Two-stage algorithm (high level)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Draw <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> samples (with <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> chosen theoretically below), compute <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and calculate</p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}_{0}:=\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}),\qquad\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}:=\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|,\qquad\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}:=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})}{\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo></msub><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}_{0}:=\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}),\qquad\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}:=\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|,\qquad\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}:=\frac{\operatorname{tr}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})}{\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using the operator-norm deviation bound (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) (with <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> plugged in) construct a lower-confidence bound</p>
<table id="S3.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda:=\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-C\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}\Big(\sqrt{\frac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda:=\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-C\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}\Big(\sqrt{\frac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Here the constant <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the same as in (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>), and the failure probability is allocated to <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi/2</annotation></semantics></math> (details below).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, increase <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. double it) and repeat until <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> set <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}:=\lambda-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}:=\lambda-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> (if <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> then <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math> was chosen too large), and then use (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) with <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma\|,\ r_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma\|,\ r_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge},\ \widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge},\ \widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math> to compute the second-stage sample size <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq C^{\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}^{2}\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\widehat{\Delta}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq C^{\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge}^{2}\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\widehat{\Delta}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The final total sample size is <math id="S3.I3.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}+K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}+K</annotation></semantics></math>, and with total failure probability <math id="S3.I3.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\xi</annotation></semantics></math> one guarantees <math id="S3.I3.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Finite-sample correctness (theoretical guarantee)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) for the first stage: if <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big)\leq\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\|\Sigma\|\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big)\leq\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then with probability at least <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\xi/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\xi/2</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)|\leq\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4},\qquad\big|\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|-\|\Sigma\|\big|\leq\frac{\|\Sigma\|}{4}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)|\leq\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4},\qquad\big|\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|-\|\Sigma\|\big|\leq\frac{\|\Sigma\|}{4}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Hence (using simple algebra <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a-a/4=3a/4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a-a/4=3a/4</annotation></semantics></math>, etc.)</p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda\geq\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4}-C\cdot\frac{5\|\Sigma\|}{4}\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big)\geq\frac{7\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{16}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mn>16</mn></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda\geq\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\frac{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{4}-C\cdot\frac{5\|\Sigma\|}{4}\Big(\sqrt{\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\frac{r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\Big)\geq\frac{7\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)}{16},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">when <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> meets (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Finite-sample correctness (theoretical guarantee) ‣ 3.5 A two-stage observable (plug-in) scheme and finite-sample guarantee ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). Thus there exists a constant <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g. <math id="S3.SS5.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime\prime}=16C^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>16</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime\prime}=16C^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>) such that (<a href="#S3.E12" title="In Finite-sample correctness (theoretical guarantee) ‣ 3.5 A two-stage observable (plug-in) scheme and finite-sample guarantee ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) is equivalent to the requirement</p>
<table id="S3.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}\geq C^{\prime\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}\geq C^{\prime\prime}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Two-stage observable estimator for the minimal prompt length</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
                
                
              tolerance <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, target <math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, initial <math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., <math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}=100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}=100</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">repeat</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
                
                
                Draw <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> demos, compute <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math>, set
<math id="alg1.l3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\lambda}_{0}=\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\lambda}_{0}=\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="alg1.l3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}=\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}=\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="alg1.l3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})/\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}})/\|\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}\|</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
                
                
                Lower confidence bound (use (<a href="#S3.E9" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>) constant <math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>):

<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\lambda}=\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-C\,\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\tfrac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msqrt><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></msqrt><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\lambda}=\widehat{\lambda}_{0}-C\,\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}\!\left(\sqrt{\tfrac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}}+\tfrac{\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{K_{0}}\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\lambda}\leq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>≤</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\lambda}\leq 0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
                
                
                 <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}\leftarrow 2K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}\leftarrow 2K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and repeat

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
                
                
                <span id="alg1.l7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">until</span> <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{\lambda}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{\lambda}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
                
                
              Set <math id="alg1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}=\underline{\lambda}-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>λ</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}=\underline{\lambda}-\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
                
                
              Compute the second-stage size using the plug-in form of (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>):

<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\;=\;\;C^{\prime}\,\frac{\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}^{2}\,\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\widehat{\Delta}^{2}}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.838em" rspace="0.838em">=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\;=\;\;C^{\prime}\,\frac{\widehat{\|\Sigma\|}^{2}\,\widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{\widehat{\Delta}^{2}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> total <math id="alg1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{final}}=K_{0}+K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>final</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{final}}=K_{0}+K</annotation></semantics></math>.

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Final implementable theorem statement</h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">[ICL Minimal Necessary Length, non-asymptotic, observable two-stage implementation]</span> Under the above setup, let <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma))</annotation></semantics></math> and failure tolerance <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ξ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>. There exist absolute constants <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{1},C_{2},C_{3}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{1},C_{2},C_{3}</annotation></semantics></math> (depending on matrix-Bernstein and sub-Gaussian constants) such that:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Non-observable bound using spectral quantities)</span> If <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta=\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq C_{1}\Big(\frac{\sigma^{4}+\|\Sigma\|^{2}}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}\log(8K/\xi)+\|\Sigma\|}{\Delta}\Big)\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq C_{1}\Big(\frac{\sigma^{4}+\|\Sigma\|^{2}}{\Delta^{2}}+\frac{\sigma^{2}\log(8K/\xi)+\|\Sigma\|}{\Delta}\Big)\log\frac{4r_{\mathrm{eff}}}{3\xi},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then <math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pr(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta)\geq 1-\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Pr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pr(\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})\geq\delta)\geq 1-\xi</annotation></semantics></math>. Ignoring lower-order terms (and using the sharper operator-norm concentration) yields the simplified dominant form</p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\geq C_{2}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\geq C_{2}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(2/\xi)}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">which suffices in practice.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Two-stage observable implementation)</span> If the first-stage sample size <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> satisfies</p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{0}\geq C_{3}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{0}\geq C_{3}\frac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}r_{\mathrm{eff}}\log(4/\xi)}{\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">then forming <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}}</annotation></semantics></math> and constructing the lower confidence bound <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E11" title="In item 2 ‣ Two-stage algorithm (high level) ‣ 3.5 A two-stage observable (plug-in) scheme and finite-sample guarantee ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>), and then setting <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{\Delta}=\lambda-\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{\Delta}=\lambda-\delta</annotation></semantics></math> and choosing <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> according to the rule in item (1) (with plug-in estimates <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge},\ \widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>r</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>eff</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma\|_{\wedge},\ \widehat{r}_{\mathrm{eff}}</annotation></semantics></math>) yields, with total failure probability <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, the guarantee <math id="S3.I4.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}+K})\geq\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K_{0}+K})\geq\delta</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.7 </span>Connection to ICL (in-context learning) and experimental suggestions</h3>

<section id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A one-shot practical calibration.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To tighten <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> for practice, we (i) replace <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma\|</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Sigma\|_{\mathrm{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Sigma\|_{\mathrm{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
(ii) use the trace-based effective rank <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{tr}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)^{2}/\|\Sigma\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo>/</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{tr}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)^{2}/\|\Sigma\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and (iii) substitute the minimum eigenvalue by a <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile eigenvalue <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{q}</annotation></semantics></math>
(<math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in[0.05,0.2]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.05</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in[0.05,0.2]</annotation></semantics></math> tuned on a small validation set; no test leakage).
We then compute</p>
<table id="S3.Ex19" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{\mathrm{cal}}\;=\;\alpha\cdot\frac{\|\widehat{\Sigma}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{\,2}\;r_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{tr}}\;\log(2/\xi)}{(\lambda_{q}-\delta)^{2}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mi>cal</mi></msup><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mi>F</mi><mn> 2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0.280em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi><mi>tr</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{\mathrm{cal}}\;=\;\alpha\cdot\frac{\|\widehat{\Sigma}\|_{\mathrm{F}}^{\,2}\;r_{\mathrm{eff}}^{\mathrm{tr}}\;\log(2/\xi)}{(\lambda_{q}-\delta)^{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where a single global scale <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is estimated once per encoder–generator pair
from a small validation subset (e.g., <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math> tasks).
This variant preserves the same monotone dependence as the theory while
substantially reducing over-conservativeness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Theoretical meaning</h5>

<div id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Using <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> as a proxy for “ICL learnability/stability” is reasonable because the condition number (and hence predictive variance in ridge regression or linear approximation) depends on inverse eigenvalues. For example, prediction variance scales with <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x^{\ast})^{\top}(H_{K}^{\top}H_{K}+\lambda I)^{-1}\phi(x^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>K</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x^{\ast})^{\top}(H_{K}^{\top}H_{K}+\lambda I)^{-1}\phi(x^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math>, which is proportional to <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(K\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})+\lambda)^{-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>λ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(K\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})+\lambda)^{-1}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> is lower bounded by <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math>, then prediction variance is upper bounded and ICL is more likely to behave stably.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Empirical validation of assumptions.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although our main theorem is derived under the nominal assumption (A1),
we conduct diagnostic experiments on hidden representations from GPT-2 models
to evaluate how well these assumptions hold in practice.
We find that (i) hidden states are approximately sub-Gaussian at intermediate layers,
though heavier tails appear in later layers;
(ii) representation drift across prompt positions is present but moderate;
(iii) the spectral statistic <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> correlates with empirical ICL stability.
These results indicate that our theoretical assumptions serve as a reasonable proxy,
and the relaxed extensions in Appendix <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Extensions Beyond the Nominal Assumption ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a> capture the observed deviations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Synthetic stress tests.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To further validate our theoretical claims, we perform controlled synthetic stress tests
(Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Synthetic Stress Tests ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>) by introducing bounded drift, heavy-tailed features,
and temporal dependence.
Across all cases, we find that empirical knee-points increase smoothly with the violation strength,
in line with our robustness extensions, confirming that the lower bound remains
a conservative but reliable predictor of ICL stability.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Remark on interpretation.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While we use <math id="S3.SS7.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> as a stability proxy,
we recognize that it can be conservative and may not fully capture
all sources of instability (e.g. attention saturation).
Appendix <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Interpretability of the Proxy ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> provides robustness analyses,
alternative spectral surrogates, and empirical diagnostics
that validate and calibrate this proxy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments to validate the theoretical lower bound on the minimal prompt length required for stable In-Context Learning (ICL). The experiments test the predictions of our theory across various datasets, encoders, and generators.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Measuring the Knee-Point</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The knee-point <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\text{knee}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>knee</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\text{knee}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as the point of minimal sum of squared errors (SSE) in a two-segment piecewise linear fit of the accuracy curve <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(K,\mathrm{ACC}(K))\}_{K\in\mathcal{K}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>ACC</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(K,\mathrm{ACC}(K))\}_{K\in\mathcal{K}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We calculate the knee-point by selecting the split index <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> that minimizes SSE, and the uncertainty is quantified via bootstrapping with 200 resamplings, reporting the 95% percentile interval for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widehat{K}_{\text{knee}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>K</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>knee</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widehat{K}_{\text{knee}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Error ratio (<math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K^{*}/\text{knee}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>/</mo><mtext>knee</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K^{*}/\text{knee}</annotation></semantics></math>) across datasets, encoders, and generators. Each number is averaged over 5 random seeds. Lower is better (closer to 1). The Raw column uses the default parameter values for prediction, whereas the Calibrated column reflects a few exploratory adjustments to some of these values; the procedure for determining each parameter is detailed in <a href="#A8.SS2" title="H.2 Theoretical Origins and Geometric Interpretation ‣ Appendix H Theoretical Constants: Sources, Mappings, and Practical Calibration ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H.2</span></a>.</figcaption>
<div id="S4.T1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:111.3pt;vertical-align:-54.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-92.9pt,30.0pt) scale(0.649928855391037,0.649928855391037) ;">
