</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Results Across Datasets</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Measuring the Knee-Point ‣ 4 Experiments ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the empirical knee-points across datasets. Theoretical predictions consistently exceed empirical knee-points with an average error ratio of 2.9×, confirming the reliability of the theoretical upper bound. The calibrated estimates (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{cal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>cal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{cal}}</annotation></semantics></math>) track the knee-point more closely.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Task-Dependence of Error Ratios</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The error ratio varies across datasets. More complex tasks, such as AGNews, require larger prompts compared to simpler tasks like TREC-6. This aligns with our theory, which predicts larger prompts for more complex tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Model Scale Effect</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the effect of model scale on ICL performance by testing a 124M and a 345M model. Larger models reduce the empirical knee-point, and the error ratio improves. For SST-2, the knee decreases from <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>12 for the 124M model to <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\approx" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>≈</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\approx</annotation></semantics></math>7 for the 345M model, with the error ratio decreasing from 3.4× to 2.2×.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.5 </span>Impact of Tokenization and Prompt Truncation</h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The choice of tokenization and prompt truncation policies significantly affects ICL stability. Single-token labels (e.g., "yes/no") lead to smaller knees and error ratios (1.2–1.5×), whereas multi-token labels (e.g., "positive/negative") result in error ratios greater than 3×. For truncation, "latest-first" truncation produces the smallest knees and error ratios (1.2–1.8×), while "earliest-first" and "random" truncations lead to larger error ratios.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.6 </span>Synthetic Stress Tests</h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further evaluate the robustness of our theoretical bound by introducing controlled violations of the nominal assumption (A1). These stress tests involve the following: <span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded Drift:</span> As the drift parameter <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> increases, the empirical knee-point increases linearly. This confirms the additive correction predicted by our robustness analysis in <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Robustness Extensions ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. <span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Heavy-Tailed Features:</span> As the tail parameter <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ν</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nu</annotation></semantics></math> decreases, the empirical knee-point increases multiplicatively. However, truncation or normalization significantly reduces this inflation, as detailed in <a href="#A4" title="Appendix D Extensions Beyond the Nominal Assumption ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D</span></a>. <span id="S4.SS6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation Studies</span>is shown in <a href="#A7" title="Appendix G Ablation Studies ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These observations confirm that the lower bound remains a reliable predictor of ICL stability across different data violations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our experiments validate an important property: theoretical predictions reliably upper bound the empirical saturation point of In-Context Learning (ICL). This provides a practical guideline for estimating the minimal number of demonstrations required for stable ICL, avoiding excessive computational costs and long sequences. This work advances the understanding of ICL’s stability by directly linking it to measurable statistical properties, offering a foundation for improving ICL performance in future work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The theoretical framework bridges high-dimensional statistical properties and ICL behavior, showing that spectral characteristics, such as minimum eigenvalue and effective rank, influence model stability. These insights lay the groundwork for refining ICL models, particularly in real-world applications. While effective across various tasks, the current analysis assumes fixed high-dimensional representations, which may limit generalization. Future work could explore relaxing this assumption and further refining calibration procedures for more complex tasks to enhance robustness and performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Looking ahead, extending this approach to multi-modal ICL and integrating adaptive prompting strategies would further improve model flexibility and stability in dynamic environments, opening new avenues for practical applications of large-scale language models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Reproducibility Statement</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We ensure reproducibility by providing detailed documentation of all hyperparameters, model architectures, and experimental setups in Appendix. All datasets and architectures used in this
paper are publicly available, and our main code is shown in supplementary material.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Ethics Statement</h2>

<div id="S7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work uses only public datasets and code under their original licenses.
No private or personally identifiable information (PII) was accessed or processed.
We audited the datasets for sensitive attributes when applicable and used them solely for research and benchmarking purposes.
Potential negative impacts mainly arise from misuse of the trained models or analyses, e.g., deploying them in high-stakes settings without additional validation.
To mitigate risks, we (i) release code and configurations for reproducibility, (ii) include usage notes that restrict deployment to research contexts, and (iii) report known failure modes and assumptions.
Computation was limited to standard academic GPUs; we estimate compute/energy use is modest relative to contemporary large-scale training, and we adopted checkpoint reuse and early stopping to reduce waste.
This statement is consistent with our LLM usage disclosure, which was limited to writing polish and literature discovery and did not involve sensitive data.</p>
</div>
</section>
