<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Use of large language models.</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We used large language models (LLMs) in two limited ways.
First, for <em id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aid or polish writing</em>, an LLM service (provider redacted for anonymous review) helped with grammar, style, and tightening of sentences.
All technical content, problem formulations, theorems, proofs, algorithmic designs, model implementations, experiments, and final claims were authored and verified by the authors.
Second, for <em id="A1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">retrieval and discovery</em>, we used LLM-assisted search to expand keyword queries and to draft short summaries of candidate papers from public abstracts.
Inclusion decisions and all citations were made only after the authors read the original sources; no autogenerated references were accepted.
No private or proprietary data were provided to the LLM, and prompts contained only our own drafts or publicly available text.
This use does not affect reproducibility: the released code, data, and results do not depend on any LLM outputs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Robustness Extensions</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main text, our theoretical guarantees are derived under the nominal assumption (A1),
namely that the representation <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is fixed and sub-Gaussian with covariance <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we discuss how the results extend when assumptions (A2)–(A4) are considered.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Robustness to Representation Drift (A2)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first allow the representation to vary with context index <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, modeled as</p>
<table id="A2.Ex20" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> uniformly in <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,t</annotation></semantics></math>.
Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}=\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}=\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math> and denote the averaged covariance by
<math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Sigma}=\tfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Sigma_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Sigma}=\tfrac{1}{T}\sum_{t=1}^{T}\Sigma_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Robustness under bounded drift</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}</annotation></semantics></math> be the sample-size lower bound under (A1), depending on <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Under (A2), the empirical covariance <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Sigma}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Sigma}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> concentrates around <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math>,
and for any <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta\in(0,\lambda_{\min}(\bar{\Sigma}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta\in(0,\lambda_{\min}(\bar{\Sigma}))</annotation></semantics></math>, with probability at least <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1-\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1-\xi</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="A2.Ex21" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})\;\geq\;\delta-c_{1}\varepsilon," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><mi>δ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})\;\geq\;\delta-c_{1}\varepsilon,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">provided</p>
<table id="A2.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\;\geq\;\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}\;+\;c_{2}\cdot\frac{\varepsilon^{2}\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.838em" rspace="0.838em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.502em">+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\;\geq\;\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}\;+\;c_{2}\cdot\frac{\varepsilon^{2}\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1},c_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1},c_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are absolute constants.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proof sketch.</h5>

<div id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Write <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> and expand
<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma_{t}=\Sigma+\mathbb{E}[\phi(x)\Delta_{t}(x)^{\top}]+\mathbb{E}[\Delta_{t}(x)\phi(x)^{\top}]+\mathbb{E}[\Delta_{t}(x)\Delta_{t}(x)^{\top}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma_{t}=\Sigma+\mathbb{E}[\phi(x)\Delta_{t}(x)^{\top}]+\mathbb{E}[\Delta_{t}(x)\phi(x)^{\top}]+\mathbb{E}[\Delta_{t}(x)\Delta_{t}(x)^{\top}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
The last three terms are bounded in operator norm by <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\varepsilon\|\Sigma\|^{1/2}+\varepsilon^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ε</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>ε</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\varepsilon\|\Sigma\|^{1/2}+\varepsilon^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Applying Weyl’s inequality gives
<math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})\geq\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-O(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})\geq\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-O(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Matrix Bernstein then yields concentration of <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Sigma}_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Sigma}_{K}</annotation></semantics></math> around <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\Sigma}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\Sigma}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same rate as before,
plus an additive slack proportional to <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
The additional sample-size term arises from controlling the variance proxy <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>-dependent terms.
∎</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Relaxing Tail Assumptions (A3)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> is only sub-exponential or has bounded fourth moments,
then <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\phi(x)\|^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\phi(x)\|^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> has heavier tails.
In this case, truncation plus Bernstein inequality yields an adjusted bound of the form</p>
<table id="A2.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\sigma^{4}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>polylog</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\sigma^{4}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is a moment constant.
The dependence on <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> reflects the heavier tails, but the overall scaling remains quadratic in the spectral gap.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Weak Dependence among Features (A4)</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are not independent across <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
we apply matrix concentration for martingale-difference or <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing sequences
(e.g., Tropp 2011, Paulin 2015).
In this case, the effective sample size is reduced:
if the mixing coefficient is <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in[0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in[0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, then the bound takes the same form as in Theorem <a href="#A2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" title="Robustness under bounded drift ‣ B.1 Robustness to Representation Drift (A2) ‣ Appendix B Robustness Extensions ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>,
with <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> replaced by <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K/(1+\rho)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K/(1+\rho)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Thus moderate dependence inflates the sample complexity by at most a constant factor.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Summary</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Together, these results demonstrate that our main lower bound is <em id="A2.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust</em>:
when the representation drifts mildly, exhibits heavier tails, or has weak temporal dependence,
the sufficient sample-size requirement increases smoothly and predictably,
rather than collapsing.
In practice, this means that the nominal bound from (A1) can still be used as a conservative guideline,
while deviations are accommodated by additive or multiplicative correction factors.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Extensions to Dependent Features</h2>

<figure id="A3.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Notation and constants used across sections.</figcaption>
<table id="A3.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A3.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt">Symbol</th>
<th id="A3.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">Meaning / Where defined</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A3.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t"><math id="A3.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma,\ \|\Sigma\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma,\ \|\Sigma\|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">Covariance and operator norm (Sec. 3)</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)/\|\Sigma\|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>tr</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\mathrm{eff}}=\mathrm{tr}(\Sigma)/\|\Sigma\|</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Effective rank (Sec. 3; Appx. G.2)</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Sub-Gaussian parameter (Appx. 3.2; Appx. G.3)</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\delta</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Target lower bound for <math id="A3.T2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\widehat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.6" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Constant in operator-norm deviation equation(9)</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.7" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime},C^{\prime\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mo>′′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime},C^{\prime\prime}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Constants in plug-in lower bounds equation(10), (13)</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.8" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row"><math id="A3.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{K}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">Truncation threshold (equation(6))</td></tr>
<tr id="A3.T2.2.9" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A3.T2.2.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><math id="A3.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{q}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{q}</annotation></semantics></math></th>
<td id="A3.T2.2.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><math id="A3.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>-quantile eigenvalue surrogate (Appx. F)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our main theorem assumes that feature vectors <math id="A3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are i.i.d. across prompt positions.
In practice, contextualized representations may exhibit correlations across <math id="A3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> due to attention and residual connections.
We now extend the analysis under weak dependence assumptions.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Martingale-difference setting</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Suppose <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a martingale-difference sequence with respect to a natural filtration
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (e.g., conditioned on past tokens).
That is, <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]=0</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then the sequence of covariance deviations</p>
<table id="A3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\;=\;\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}-\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\;=\;\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}-\mathbb{E}[\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}\mid\mathcal{F}_{t-1}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">is a matrix martingale-difference sequence.
By applying the matrix Azuma–Hoeffding inequality (Tropp 2011; Hsu et al. 2012), we obtain concentration of
<math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\Sigma}_{K}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{t=1}^{K}\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>K</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>K</mi></msubsup><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\Sigma}_{K}=\tfrac{1}{K}\sum_{t=1}^{K}\phi_{t}(x)\phi_{t}(x)^{\top}</annotation></semantics></math> around its expectation,
with variance proxy and tail probability essentially the same as in the i.i.d. case,
up to a constant factor.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lower bound under martingale differences</h5>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption (A4) with bounded martingale differences,
the non-asymptotic lower bound on <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> in Theorem 3.1 remains valid
up to replacing <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\cdot\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\cdot\xi</annotation></semantics></math> for an absolute constant <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Mixing sequences</h3>

<div id="A3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Alternatively, suppose <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> is <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing with coefficient <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Following Paulin (2015) and Merlevède et al. (2009),
matrix Bernstein inequalities for mixing sequences yield concentration with an effective sample size</p>
<table id="A3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{eff}}\;\approx\;\frac{K}{1+c_{\rho}}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≈</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{eff}}\;\approx\;\frac{K}{1+c_{\rho}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}=\sum_{s\geq 1}\alpha(s)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}=\sum_{s\geq 1}\alpha(s)</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies temporal dependence.
In particular, geometric mixing (<math id="A3.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><msup><mi>ρ</mi><mi>s</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha(s)\leq\rho^{s}</annotation></semantics></math>) gives <math id="A3.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>ρ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}=O(1/(1-\rho))</annotation></semantics></math>,
so that moderate dependence inflates the required prompt length by at most a constant factor.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Lower bound under mixing</h5>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under Assumption (A4) with mixing coefficient <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math>,
the sufficient condition on prompt length becomes</p>
<table id="A3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;(1+c_{\rho})\cdot\frac{\sigma^{2}\,\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\log\!\frac{d}{\xi}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><mo lspace="0.337em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;(1+c_{\rho})\cdot\frac{\sigma^{2}\,\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\log\!\frac{d}{\xi}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Discussion</h3>

<div id="A3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">These results show that our main conclusions are <em id="A3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust to weak dependence</em>.
Even if contextualized features <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\phi_{t}(x)\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\phi_{t}(x)\}</annotation></semantics></math> are not independent,
as long as dependence decays over time (mixing) or is conditionally centered (martingale-difference),
the sufficient sample size bound degrades only by a multiplicative factor,
rather than failing altogether.
In practice, this suggests that <math id="A3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> can still serve as a useful stability proxy,
with dependence merely shifting the knee-point moderately to the right.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Extensions Beyond the Nominal Assumption</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the main paper we derived our non-asymptotic lower bound under the nominal assumption (A1),
namely that representations <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> are fixed, i.i.d., and sub-Gaussian with covariance <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here we provide extensions under weaker assumptions (A2)–(A4), complementing Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Robustness Extensions ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Extensions to Dependent Features ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.
Overall, these results show that our theory is <em id="A4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">robust</em>:
the sufficient prompt length bound degrades smoothly under moderate violations of (A1),
rather than failing abruptly.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Representation Drift (A2)</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As discussed in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Robustness Extensions ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>,
if the representation depends on the context index <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, modeled as
<math id="A4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|_{2}\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>,
then eigenvalue perturbation bounds (Weyl’s inequality)
imply that <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is reduced by at most <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="O(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">O(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>.
Combining this with matrix Bernstein yields a lower bound of the form</p>
<table id="A4.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">with <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> polynomial in <math id="A4.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and spectral ratios.
Hence, small drift only shifts the required prompt length slightly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Relaxed Tail Assumptions (A3)</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(x)</annotation></semantics></math> has heavier tails than sub-Gaussian—e.g., only sub-exponential
or bounded fourth moments—then truncation and Bernstein’s inequality (Vershynin, 2018)
give concentration at a slightly slower rate.
In this case, the sufficient prompt length becomes</p>
<table id="A4.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>polylog</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;\frac{(\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2})}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> is a tail parameter depending on the fourth moment or sub-exponential constant.
Thus, the order of the bound remains unchanged, but the constant is inflated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>Weak Dependence (A4)</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When features are not independent across <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>,
we can use concentration for martingale-difference sequences (Tropp, 2011)
or <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing processes (Paulin, 2015; Merlevède et al., 2009).
Both cases yield an effective sample size of the form
<math id="A4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{eff}}=K/(1+c_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{eff}}=K/(1+c_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{\rho}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{\rho}</annotation></semantics></math> is a dependence factor.
Thus, weak dependence inflates the sufficient prompt length by a multiplicative constant,
but does not alter the overall scaling.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Summary of Extensions</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The results under (A2)–(A4) can be summarized as follows:</p>
<ul id="A4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Drift in representations adds an additive correction <math id="A4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>, linear or quadratic in the drift magnitude.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Heavy-tailed features require an inflated constant, governed by moment parameters, but retain the same quadratic dependence on the spectral gap.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Temporal dependence reduces the effective sample size, inflating the required <math id="A4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> by a constant factor depending on mixing strength.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Taken together, these extensions demonstrate that the main non-asymptotic bound is conservative but stable:
it remains meaningful even when real-world deviations from (A1) occur,
providing a useful guideline for interpreting empirical ICL stability.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A4.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Summary of assumptions and their effect on the sufficient prompt length <math id="A4.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>.
Here <math id="A4.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the bound under (A1).</figcaption>
<table id="A4.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="A4.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A4.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T3.2.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Assumption</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A4.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:148.0pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T3.2.1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Description</span></span>
</span></th>
<th id="A4.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.1.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.T3.2.1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Effect on lower bound</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="A4.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">(A1) Nominal</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:148.0pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Fixed sub-Gaussian features with covariance <math id="A4.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sigma</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=O\!\left(\tfrac{\sigma^{2}\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\log\!\tfrac{d}{\xi}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mpadded style="width:0.621em;" width="0.621em"><mi>O</mi></mpadded><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mi>ξ</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=O\!\left(\tfrac{\sigma^{2}\|\Sigma\|}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\log\!\tfrac{d}{\xi}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">(A2) Drift</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:148.0pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Context-dependent features
<math id="A4.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}(x)=\phi(x)+\Delta_{t}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="A4.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|\leq\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\Delta_{t}(x)\|\leq\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\geq\;K_{\mathrm{nominal}}+g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math>,
where <math id="A4.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g(\varepsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ε</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g(\varepsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> grows polynomially in <math id="A4.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and spectral ratios</span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">(A3) Heavy tails</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:148.0pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Sub-exponential or bounded fourth moments, parameter <math id="A4.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="A4.T3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K\;\gtrsim\;\tfrac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2}}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0.558em" rspace="0.558em">≳</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>κ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>δ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>polylog</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>ξ</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K\;\gtrsim\;\tfrac{\|\Sigma\|^{2}+\kappa^{2}}{(\lambda_{\min}(\Sigma)-\delta)^{2}}\,\mathrm{polylog}(d/\xi)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A4.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A4.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:71.1pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p">(A4) Dependence</span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:148.0pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Features form a martingale-difference sequence or <math id="A4.T3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-mixing process with coefficient <math id="A4.T3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A4.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_bb">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:184.9pt;">
<span id="A4.T3.2.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p">Effective sample size
<math id="A4.T3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{eff}}\approx\tfrac{K}{1+c_{\rho}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>eff</mi></msub><mo>≈</mo><mfrac><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{eff}}\approx\tfrac{K}{1+c_{\rho}}</annotation></semantics></math>,
so bound inflates by factor <math id="A4.T3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1+c_{\rho})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>ρ</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1+c_{\rho})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
