<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h5>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical kurtosis values are close to the Gaussian baseline (3.0) for short prompts
but increase with <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting heavier tails at longer contexts, consistent with Assumption (A3).
The drift statistic <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D(K)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(K)</annotation></semantics></math> grows gradually, supporting Assumption (A2) with small but non-negligible <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ε</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon</annotation></semantics></math>.
The spectral statistic <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi mathvariant="normal">Σ</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\min}(\hat{\Sigma}_{K})</annotation></semantics></math> decreases as <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> increases,
and its trend correlates with the empirical knee-point in ICL accuracy.
Bootstrap confidence intervals confirm that these trends are statistically significant.
Overall, the diagnostics support our claim that the nominal assumption (A1)
is a reasonable approximation in practice, while the relaxed extensions (A2)–(A4)
capture the observed deviations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Synthetic Stress Tests</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the robustness of our theoretical bound under controlled violations of Assumption (A1),
we conducted synthetic stress tests along three axes:
(i) bounded drift in representations (A2),
(ii) heavy-tailed features (A3),
and (iii) temporal dependence (A4).
For each setting we generated synthetic features and tasks as described in Section <a href="#A7.SS8" title="G.8 Synthetic Data Experiments ‣ Appendix G Ablation Studies ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.8</span></a>,
measured empirical knee-points <math id="A5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> of in-context learning performance,
and compared them against the theoretical lower bound <math id="A5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Bounded Drift</h3>

<figure id="A5.T5" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Drift stress test results (<math id="A5.T5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=50</annotation></semantics></math>).
<math id="A5.T5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mi>nominal</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>32</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\mathrm{nominal}}=32</annotation></semantics></math> computed by  (<a href="#S3.E10" title="In 3.4 A more concise, commonly used operator-norm concentration (avoid truncation and the self-consistency) ‣ 3 Setup and Notation (re-stated for convenience) ‣ Theoretical Bounds for Stable In-Context Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>).
<math id="A5.T5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K_{\text{emp}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>K</mi><mtext>emp</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K_{\text{emp}}</annotation></semantics></math> is reported with 95% bootstrap CI.</figcaption>
